<?xml version='1.0' encoding='utf-8'?>

<section xml:id="sec_capstone_integration_overview" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Integrating Subsystems for Competition Robotics</title>
  <introduction>
    <p>This chapter is a capstone lab experience that combines the perception, planning, and control ideas from earlier chapters into two applied challenges: a soccer competition and a cargo-sorting task. Neither challenge introduces a brand-new algorithm. Instead, each asks you to combine kinematics, reactive avoidance, path planning, and vision-based classification into one working robot behavior under time pressure and imperfect sensing.</p>
  </introduction>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec_capstone_subsystem_map">
    <title>Mapping Prior Chapters onto Competition Tasks</title>
    <ul>
      <li><p><em class="emphasis">Kinematics:</em> converting desired linear and angular velocity into wheel commands so the robot chases a ball or transports cargo smoothly.</p></li>
      <li><p><em class="emphasis">Path planning and reactive avoidance:</em> choosing a route to a ball, goal, or bin while avoiding opponents, teammates, and static obstacles.</p></li>
      <li><p><em class="emphasis">Vision and classification:</em> identifying the ball, goal, or cargo type from camera input to decide what action comes next.</p></li>
      <li><p><em class="emphasis">State machines:</em> sequencing behaviors such as search, approach, align, and act (kick or place) so the robot does not attempt two goals at once.</p></li>
    </ul>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec_capstone_evaluation_pressure">
    <title>Why Integration Is Harder Than Any Single Part</title>
    <p>Each subsystem you built earlier was tested in isolation with clean assumptions. In competition, sensor noise, timing delays, and a moving opponent or shifting cargo pile all interact at once. A path planner that is normally reliable can produce a stale route if perception updates lag behind robot motion. This chapter asks you to reason about these interactions rather than treat each subsystem as independent.</p>
  </subsection>
</section>
